Methods to Plot Multiple Graphs on the Same Plot:

--- Method 1: hold on / hold off ---
Keeps existing plot and adds new plots on top.
  x = 0:0.1:2*pi;
  plot(x, sin(x), 'r-');   % plot sine in red
  hold on;
  plot(x, cos(x), 'b--');  % add cosine in blue dashed
  hold off;
  legend('sin(x)', 'cos(x)');
  title('Sine and Cosine');

--- Method 2: Multiple data in one plot() call ---
  x = 0:0.1:2*pi;
  plot(x, sin(x), 'r-', x, cos(x), 'b--', x, tan(x), 'g:');
  legend('sin', 'cos', 'tan');
  axis([0 2*pi -2 2]);

--- Method 3: subplot() for multiple panels ---
  subplot(2,1,1);           % 2 rows, 1 col, position 1
  plot(x, sin(x));
  title('Sine');
  subplot(2,1,2);           % position 2
  plot(x, cos(x));
  title('Cosine');

Differences:

| Feature | hold on | plot(x,y,x,y,...) | subplot |
|---|---|---|---|
| Graphs share axes | Yes | Yes | No (separate axes) |
| Added incrementally | Yes | No (all at once) | N/A |
| Code clarity | Good for iteration/loops | Concise for few curves | Best for comparison |
| Zoom/pan effect | Same for all | Same for all | Independent |
| Best use | Dynamic/loop plotting | 2-3 curves at once | Side-by-side comparison |

Key Commands:
  legend() — adds labels for each curve
  grid on  — adds grid lines
  xlabel(), ylabel() — axis labels
  title()  — plot title

